1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,586
Before a Zone business releases a withdrawal batch,

2
00:00:03,636 --> 00:00:08,132
an auditor or settlement counterparty may need to check it.

3
00:00:08,182 --> 00:00:13,132
But the operator holds the private ledger and the batch witness.

4
00:00:13,182 --> 00:00:19,950
Sworn creates independently verifiable, audit-ready evidence for that exact batch, without exposing customer transaction contents.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,462
The operator selects the batch in Sworn's local console and starts a proof job.

6
00:00:26,512 --> 00:00:31,578
Sworn runs Tempo's own Zone verification code in a zero-knowledge VM,

7
00:00:31,628 --> 00:00:37,159
then creates proof evidence that a third party can verify on chain.

8
00:00:37,209 --> 00:00:39,950
The private transaction contents stay private.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,693
We have done this on Moderato:

10
00:00:42,743 --> 00:00:49,093
a test batch with one withdrawal and two user transactions verified by a contract.

11
00:00:49,143 --> 00:00:51,836
Change one input, and verification fails.

12
00:00:51,886 --> 00:00:55,950
This is a working proof pipeline, not a mockup.

13
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:04,283
The buyer we are pursuing is a Zone business that must give a counterparty confidence before it settles.

14
00:01:04,333 --> 00:01:07,524
It would pay Sworn for two things:

15
00:01:07,574 --> 00:01:13,543
proof generation for each batch it supplies, and compatibility maintenance through Tempo upgrades.

16
00:01:13,593 --> 00:01:17,246
That is the contract we want to test.

17
00:01:17,296 --> 00:01:20,950
We will validate the demand with design partners.

18
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,246
Generic proving tools run programs.

19
00:01:23,296 --> 00:01:26,919
They do not deliver a Tempo-specific evidence operation.

20
00:01:26,969 --> 00:01:32,889
Sworn already runs Tempo's code, binds to the batch inputs its portal understands,

21
00:01:32,939 --> 00:01:36,103
and is built to track execution upgrades.

22
00:01:36,153 --> 00:01:43,450
I work on Reth and Revm, teach that stack, and have fifteen years building banking systems.

23
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:46,256
Today: testnet, unaudited, Tempo integration-test batch.

24
00:01:46,306 --> 00:01:47,660
No customer claimed.

25
00:01:47,710 --> 00:01:55,144
Next, a design partner supplies a batch and decides if independent proof is worth paying for.

26
00:01:55,194 --> 00:01:57,950
Sworn: audit-ready proof for private execution.
